{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "component_id": "concern_001",
      "text": "When an employee is hired on probation, the employer must, at the time of engagement, clearly inform the employee of the specific standards he must meet to qualify as a regular employee; termination is allowed only for a just cause or if the employee fails to meet those reasonable, pre‑communicated standards.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote_id": "concern_001.s1.q4"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "concern_002",
      "text": "An express warranty must include a stated period during which the warranty is enforceable; the seller and consumer may agree on that period, and any clause that tries to omit or waive the warranty period is declared null and void under the anti‑waiver provision.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote_id": "concern_002.s1.q34"
        },
        {
          "quote_id": "concern_002.s1.q26"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "concern_003",
      "text": "To enforce a warranty, the buyer presents the product with the warranty card or receipt to the immediate seller; if that seller is a retailer (not the distributor or manufacturer), the retailer must forward the claim to the distributor at no cost to the buyer and is only subsidiarily liable if both upstream parties fail. The source does not provide rules on the employment question.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote_id": "concern_003.s1.q19"
        },
        {
          "quote_id": "concern_003.s1.q20"
        },
        {
          "quote_id": "concern_003.s1.q24"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "component_id": "concern_001",
      "text": "For a covered employee, the employer must tell the employee at the start what standards will be used to decide if he becomes regular. The probation period is limited to six months unless an apprenticeship allows longer, and dismissal is allowed only for a just cause, when authorized by law, or if the employee fails to meet the reasonable standards that were disclosed.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote_id": "concern_001.s1.q3"
        },
        {
          "quote_id": "concern_001.s1.q4"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "concern_002",
      "text": "The Consumer Act requires an express warranty to state the period during which it can be enforced. The seller and buyer may agree on that period, but any warranty that omits the duration is contrary to the law and is therefore void.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote_id": "concern_002.s1.q34"
        },
        {
          "quote_id": "concern_002.s1.q26"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "concern_003",
      "text": "To enforce a warranty, the consumer only needs to present the product together with the warranty card or receipt to the immediate seller, who may not demand other documents. If the seller is an ordinary retailer, it must forward the claim to the distributor at no cost to the buyer; the retailer is only subsidiarily liable if both the manufacturer and distributor fail to honor the warranty.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote_id": "concern_003.s1.q19"
        },
        {
          "quote_id": "concern_003.s1.q20"
        },
        {
          "quote_id": "concern_003.s1.q24"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "component_id": "concern_001",
      "text": "The rule says every employer must give employees a payroll that lists, among other things, the length of time the pay covers. It does not set a mandatory pay period such as weekly or bi‑weekly; it only requires the employer to state what period is being paid for in the payroll.",
