{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "component_id": "concern_001",
      "text": "Under Section 14 of the Omnibus Rules, an employer may deduct from an employee’s wages for loss or damage only if (a) the employee is clearly shown to be responsible for the loss, (b) the employee is given a reasonable opportunity to show cause why the deduction should not be made, (c) the amount deducted is fair, reasonable and does not exceed the actual loss, and (d) the deduction does not exceed 20 percent of the employee’s weekly wages. If the private business does not first establish responsibility or give that opportunity, the deduction would fail these conditions and could be deemed an unlawful withholding under Art. 116 of the Labor Code, which prohibits any wage withholding without the worker’s consent, and also an unlawful deduction under Art. 117, which bars deductions made for the employer’s benefit.",
      "evidence": [
        { "quote_id": "concern_001.s1.q2" },
